.\" kge(1) - Kyle's Graphical Editor
.\"
.\" The keybinding sections are copied from kte.1; keep the two in step.
.TH KGE 1 "2026-11-17" "kte 1.14.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kge \- Kyle's Text Editor, graphical version
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kge
.RI [ options ]
.RI [[+ line ]
.IR file " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kge
is the graphical version of
.BR kte (1).
It has the same commands and keybindings and adds color themes, a
choice of built-in fonts, and multiple windows. It is built with SDL2,
OpenGL and Dear ImGui.
.PP
Each
.I file
named on the command line opens in its own buffer. A file that does not
exist yet is created when you first save it. An argument of the form
.BI + N
moves the cursor to line
.I N
of the file that follows it. With no files,
.B kge
starts with an empty buffer.
.PP
On macOS
.B kge
is usually installed as the application
.IR kge.app .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-gui
Open a window. This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-term
Run in the terminal instead, exactly like
.BR kte (1).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH NOTATION
.B C-x
means hold Control and press x.
.B ESC x
means press Escape and then x; holding Alt (Meta) while pressing x does
the same thing.
.B C-k x
means press Control-K, let go, and then press x. Hold Control on the
second key only where the list below says so (for example
.BR "C-k C-d" ,
which differs from
.BR "C-k d" ).
ESC or
.B C-g
abandons a half-typed K-command.
.SH K-COMMANDS
.TP
.B C-k SPACE
Set or clear the mark.
.TP
.B C-k \(aq
Toggle read-only for the current buffer.
.TP
.B C-k \-
Unindent the region (the mark must be set).
.TP
.B C-k =
Indent the region (the mark must be set).
.TP
.B C-k /
Toggle visual-line mode, which edits several lines at once.
.TP
.B C-k ;
Open the command prompt; see
.B COMMAND PROMPT
below.
.TP
.B C-k a
Set the mark at the start of the file and move to the end, selecting
everything.
.TP
.B C-k b
Switch to a buffer by name.
.TP
.B C-k c
Close the current buffer. Closing the last buffer leaves an empty one;
use
.B C-k q
to leave the editor.
.TP
.B C-k d
Delete from the cursor to the end of the line.
.TP
.B C-k C-d
Delete the whole line.
.TP
.B C-k e
Open a file.
.TP
.B C-k f
Empty the kill ring.
.TP
.B C-k g
Go to a line number.
.TP
.B C-k h
Show the built-in help in a read-only +HELP+ buffer.
.TP
.B C-k i
Open a new, empty buffer.
.TP
.B C-k j
Jump to the mark.
.TP
.B C-k k
Scroll so the cursor's line is in the middle of the screen.
.TP
.B C-k l
Reload the current buffer from disk and redraw the screen.
.TP
.B C-k m
Switch the buffer between code and writing mode, which changes the
font; see
.BR "FONTS AND EDIT MODES" .
.TP
.B C-k n
Switch to the previous buffer.
.TP
.B C-k o
Change the working directory.
.TP
.B C-k p
Switch to the next buffer.
.TP
.B C-k q
Quit. If a buffer has unsaved changes you are warned, and a second
.B C-k q
quits anyway.
.TP
.B C-k C-q
Quit at once, without asking.
.TP
.B C-k r
Redo.
.TP
.BR "C-k s" ", " "C-k C-s"
Save the buffer, asking for a file name if it has none.
.TP
.B C-k u
Undo.
.TP
.B C-k v
Toggle the file picker.
.TP
.B C-k w
Show the working directory.
.TP
.BR "C-k x" ", " "C-k C-x"
Save and quit.
.TP
.B C-k y
Yank (paste) from the kill ring.
.SH OTHER KEYS
.SS Moving
.TP
.BR C-f ", " C-b ", " C-n ", " C-p
Right, left, down, up. The arrow keys do the same.
.TP
.BR C-a ", " C-e
Start and end of the line (also Home and End).
.TP
.BR "ESC f" ", " "ESC b"
Next and previous word.
.TP
.BR "ESC <" ", " "ESC >"
Start and end of the file.
.PP
Page Up and Page Down move a screen at a time.
.SS Editing
.TP
.B Enter
Start a new line, indented to match the current one.
.TP
.BR "ESC Enter" ", " Shift+Enter
Start a new line with no indentation.
.TP
.B C-d
Delete the character under the cursor (also Delete).
.TP
.B ESC d
Delete the next word.
.TP
.B ESC BACKSPACE
Delete the previous word.
.TP
.B ESC q
Reflow the paragraph to 72 columns, or to the width given with
.BR C-u .
.SS The mark and the kill ring
.TP
.B ESC m
Set or clear the mark (same as
.BR "C-k SPACE" ).
The text between the mark and the cursor is the region.
.TP
.B C-w
Cut (kill) the region.
.TP
.B ESC w
Copy the region to the kill ring.
.TP
.B C-y
Yank (paste) from the kill ring.
.SS Searching
.TP
.B C-s
Incremental search. The down and right arrows go to the next match;
up and left go to the previous one.
.TP
.B C-r
Regular-expression search.
.TP
.B C-h
Search and replace.
.TP
.B C-t
Regular-expression search and replace.
.SS Everything else
.TP
.B C-u
Universal argument: type
.B C-u
and a number, and the next command repeats that many times.
.B C-u
on its own means 4.
.TP
.B C-g
Cancel the current prompt or operation.
.TP
.B C-l
Redraw the screen.
.SS Window keys
On macOS use Cmd where these say Ctrl.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+= ", " Ctrl+\- ", " Ctrl+0
Make the text bigger, smaller, or back to the
.B size
in the configuration file.
.TP
.B Ctrl+V
Paste from the system clipboard. Text you cut or copy with
.BR C-w ,
.B ESC w
and the other kill commands also goes to the system clipboard.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+N
Open another window (Cmd+N on macOS). All windows share the same
buffers. Closing a secondary window leaves the others open; closing the
first window quits the editor.
.PP
The mouse wheel scrolls, and clicking or dragging moves the cursor and
selects text.
.SH COMMAND PROMPT
.B C-k ;
opens a prompt at the bottom of the screen. Type a command and press
Enter. Tab completes command names and their arguments.
.TP
.BR syntax " " on | off | reload
Turn syntax highlighting on or off for the buffer, or rescan it.
.TP
.BI "set filetype=" type
Choose the highlighter by hand. Known types: cpp, go, python, rust,
shell, json, markdown, lisp, sql, erlang and forth (common file
extensions such as c, py and sh are accepted too).
.TP
.BR mode " [" code | writing ]
Switch, or set, the buffer's edit mode.
.TP
.BI "theme " name
Switch to another color theme; see
.BR THEMES .
.TP
.BR background " " light | dark
Choose the light or dark version of themes that have both.
.TP
.BI "font\-size " pixels
Set the text size until you quit.
.SH FONTS AND EDIT MODES
Every buffer is in one of two edit modes, and each mode has its own
font.
.B Code
mode uses a monospace font and is the default.
.B Writing
mode uses a proportional font and no syntax highlighting. Files
ending in .txt, .md, .markdown, .rst, .org, .tex, .adoc or .asciidoc
open in writing mode. Switch modes with
.B C-k m
or
.BR ":\ mode" .
.PP
These fonts are built in; you do not need them installed:
.PP
.RS
Monospace: b612, berkeley, berkeley\-bold, brassmono,
brassmono\-bold, brassmonocode, brassmonocode\-bold, fira, go, ibm,
idealist, inconsolata, inconsolataex, iosevka, iosevkaex, sharetech,
space, syne, triplicate, unispace.
.PP
Proportional: crimsonpro, etbook, spectral.
.PP
.B default
is Berkeley Mono Bold, the same as berkeley\-bold.
.RE
.PP
Choose fonts in the configuration file below.
.SH THEMES
amber, eink, everforest, gruvbox, kanagawa\-paper, lcars, leuchtturm,
nord, old\-book, orbital, plan9, solarized, tufte, weyland\-yutani and
zenburn. The default is nord.
.PP
eink, gruvbox, kanagawa\-paper, leuchtturm, old\-book, solarized and
tufte have light and dark versions, chosen with
.BR ":\ background"
or the
.B background
setting. Names such as gruvbox\-light or solarized\-dark pick the theme
and the background in one go.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B kge
reads
.I ~/.config/kte/kge.toml
when it starts. Every setting is optional. A complete file looks like
this:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[window]
fullscreen = false
columns = 80
rows = 42

[font]
name = "default"
size = 18.0
code = "default"
writing = "crimsonpro"

[appearance]
theme = "nord"
background = "dark"

[editor]
syntax = true
.fi
.RE
.SS [window]
.TP
.B fullscreen
true to start full screen. Default false.
.TP
.BR columns ", " rows
The starting window size in characters. Defaults 80 and 42; values
outside 1 to 1000 are ignored.
.SS [font]
.TP
.B name
The base font, which
.B code
and
.B writing
fall back to. Default "default".
.TP
.B size
The text size in pixels. Default 18; values outside 4 to 200 are
ignored.
.TP
.B code
The font for code mode. If you leave it out, it follows
.BR name .
.TP
.B writing
The font for writing mode. Default "crimsonpro"; if you leave it out
and set
.B name
to something other than "default", writing mode uses
.B name
too.
.PP
Each buffer is drawn in the font for its edit mode, so
.B name
matters only through those defaults. To use one font everywhere, set
only
.BR name .
To keep a serif font for prose, set
.B writing
as well.
.SS [appearance]
.TP
.B theme
The color theme. Default "nord".
.TP
.B background
"dark" (the default) or "light".
.SS [editor]
.TP
.B syntax
false to start with syntax highlighting off. Default true.
.PP
If
.I kge.toml
is missing or cannot be parsed (the error is printed),
.B kge
falls back to the older
.I ~/.config/kte/kge.ini
format, whose keys are fullscreen, columns, rows, font, font_size,
code_font, writing_font, theme, background and syntax, written as
.IR key = value .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/kte/kge.toml
The configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/kte/swap/
Crash-recovery journals, one per open file. If the editor dies with
unsaved changes, the next time you open that file you are asked whether
to recover them. Set
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
to move this directory.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/kte/error.log
Errors the editor has logged.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
The terminal type, used with
.BR \-\-term .
.TP
.BR LANG ", " LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE
The locale; use a UTF-8 locale to edit non-ASCII text.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the swap journals live (default
.IR ~/.local/state ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 2 for a bad option; non-zero on other failures.
.SH EXAMPLES
Edit two files in one window:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kge main.cc Buffer.cc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Edit in the terminal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kge \-\-term README.md
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kte (1)
.PP
The source and releases are at https://github.com/kisom/kte.
